    "https://jobs.uca.edu/postings/19991": {
      "title": "Police Officer-Sergeant-UCA Internal Hire ONLY",
      "description": "University of Central Arkansas Employment Opportunities | Police Officer-Sergeant-UCA Internal Hire ONLY Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs NEW Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Police Officer-Sergeant-UCA Internal Hire ONLY Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Title Information Full-Time/Part-Time Full Time Salary Range $55,141 Will this position be Onsite, Remote, or Hybrid? Onsite Position Details Position Information Posting Number NC1442026 Working Title Police Officer-Sergeant-UCA Internal Hire ONLY Department Org 123000 - University Police Why should you work at UCA? We are excited that you are considering joining the UCA Family—the faculty, staff, students and alumni—who make the University of Central Arkansas a great place to work! At the University of Central Arkansas, we value our employees striving to maintain a work-life balance. We realize that this affects the overall well-being of employees while learning to prioritize challenging work and the ability to make a difference. Participation in university-wide flexible work schedules may be available for certain positions. Training is provided on systems or job-specific software, and tools are available supporting professional and personal development. The benefits package allows you to customize your benefits to meet your current needs, with the ability to adjust them as your priorities change. This includes university contributions to health, dental, life and disability insurance, 80% tuition waivers for employees and their families, 12 official holidays, immediate leave accrual, and a retirement program with university contributions of 10% of employee salary. Also available are health and wellness incentives, as well as access to sports and cultural activities on campus for the family, free gym membership and equipment rentals. Position Summary Supervises a public safety staff consisting of certified officers and other security personnel by preparing and assigning work schedules, monitoring work progress of subordinate staff, and evaluating job performance. Reviews various reports and documentation submitted by subordinate staff for accuracy and completion and makes revisions as needed. Compiles statistics for departmental and special reports, as required, coordinates or assists in the planning and implementing of security for special events. Conducts special investigations and responds to more complex and difficult public safety situations, informs staff of changes in laws, regulations, policies and procedures. Conducts investigations of crimes, accidents, and reports incidents committed on campus. Attends departmental staff meetings, provides information, and makes recommendations on work area operations. May patrol assigned areas to detect and prevent crimes, enforce traffic laws, recognize parking violations, issue traffic citations, and identify safety hazards and violators. Performs other duties as assigned. Job Duties and Responsibilities Conducts foot and vehicle patrol of assigned areas to detect and prevent crimes, enforce laws and regulations, protect life and property, preserve order, and to make inquiries regarding reported or observed incidents. Enforces federal, state and local laws and University rules and regulations. Arrests and restrains persons who violate laws and ordinances. Identifies suspects, accompanies suspects to headquarters, jail or court and appears in court as the arresting officer or expert witness. Applies the concept and philosophy of Community-Oriented Policing in the daily discharge of all duties and in meeting all priorities assigned to the Patrol Services Division. Supports the University judicial process, reports criminal and misconduct incidents to appropriate University officials, defers offenders through the University judicial process when appropriate, and appears in campus judicial proceedings as the arresting/reporting officer. Enforces state traffic laws and University traffic and parking regulations, issues university and municipal citations and warnings, impounds vehicles, and directs traffic as needed. Responds to and attends fire and medical emergencies, provides all possible assistance, administers first aid, and prepares necessary reports. Intervenes in crisis or conflict situations. Evaluates circumstances, coordinates outside responses, and secures hazardous areas. Conducts preliminary and follow-up investigation of criminal and other incidents occurring on University property which includes interviewing and interrogating suspects, witnesses, and victims; identifying perpetrators; seeking arrest warrants; and completing investigative reports. Performs security and safety checks of University facilities to ensure a safe and healthy environment. Identifies safety hazards and physical plant emergencies. Secures and provides authorized access to University facilities during hours of operation and closure. Takes appropriate action in response to intrusion and panic alarms. Provides information and assistance to students, staff, faculty and the general public. Delivers emergency messages, provides motorist assistance services, and performs other appropriate courtesy services for the general public. Gives advice on laws and regulations and provides general information regarding the criminal justice system to the public. Represents the Department in conducting crime prevention presentations and surveys, and conducts workshops on a variety of issues regarding campus safety and security. Provides security for special events, dances and organizational events, entertainment, government officials, and athletic contests held on campus or sponsored by appropriate University officials. Prepares technical and legal reports such as crime, special, medical and incident reports. Maintains records and log books as required. Maintains current knowledge of police and public safety methods and practices; criminal law; laws of arrest, search and seizure; self-defense; and traffic enforcement. Assigns officers, monitors the progress of officers in the proper disposition of calls, reports and assigned tasks; directs shift/team activities; and ensures completion of all functions and tasks assigned to the shift/team. Ensures that shift/team priorities and objectives are achieved on a regular basis. Ensures officers under their command carry all required equipment. Supervises patrol, traffic, and special assignment police officers during an assigned shift; monitors all police activity on campus, including police patrol, intervention in domestic violence situations, apprehending criminal suspects, making arrests, investigating crimes, gathering intelligence, collecting evidence, enforcing laws, monitoring traffic and assisting citizens. Performs the full range of duties of subordinate officers when necessary for the efficient, effective, and timely operation of the Police Department; responds to calls, investigates cases, writes reports and makes arrests; resolves operational problems and questions arising from staff and citizens. Serves as the senior ranking officer on an assigned shift responsible for the proper communication of information received from command personnel, other shift/team sergeants, university offices, and the off-going shift. Assumes responsibility for directing departmental response to critical incidents occurring on a shift until and unless relieved of command by higher authority. Performs the more difficult or critical tasks related to the shift. Supervises and evaluates the performance of assigned police officers, maintains morale within a team and/or on a shift, and ensures adherence to Department and University policy; resolves, or if required, refers to higher authority, shift/team problems; provides for on-going shift training and assists the department training officer with specialized and in-service training programs. May be assigned to supervise programs such as field training and evaluation program, rangemaster, training, bike team, community policing services, crime prevention, critical response team, etc. In such cases, the Patrol Sergeant is responsible for program planning, development, implementation, and delivery of services. As the senior officer on a shift, provides site supervision of all Police Department divisions and functions in the absence of higher authority and/or division supervision. Submits to higher authority suggestions that enhance Department operations, programs or functions. Represents the interest and well-being of non-command personnel to higher authority and transmits management concerns and directives from command personnel. Reviews reports submitted by patrol officers; ensures completeness, accuracy and timely submission of crime, incident, accident, and related police reports; reviews shift logs to ensure that they are complete, thorough and accurate; reviews activity logs to ensure that officers are productive; performs staff studies as required; writes reports and memoranda; responds to the public regarding shift/team operations; and consistently promotes the mission of the Department. Coordinates special event security on an assigned shift; maintains records and log books as required; performs crime prevention and awareness functions; and reports equipment condition and repair needs to higher authority. Advises and assists assigned personnel in the proper disposition of complex or difficult patrol situations and is accountable for the work product of officer. Conducts investigations of citizen complaints and personnel misconduct in accordance with Department policy or as assigned by higher authority and makes appropriate recommendations to the Patrol Services Division Commander. Maintains current knowledge of police and public safety methods and practices; criminal law; laws of arrest, search and seizure; self-defense; and traffic enforcement. Maintains accurate and up-to-date time sheets for patrol officers. Performs other related duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Must be able to work rotating shifts and days off with routine overtime requirements and frequently work early and/or late hours; must be willing to work inside and outside in extreme weather conditions; must be able to use physical force to intercede when necessary. The incumbent must be available to respond to critical incidents on the campus 24 hours/seven days a week. Knowledge of federal, state and local laws and University regulations, including rules of criminal procedure, laws governing search and seizure and rules of evidence. Knowledge of modern police methods. Knowledge of the principles of management. Ability to organize the work activities of others. Knowledge of modern police patrol methods and practices. Knowledge of techniques and procedures used in the successful investigation of criminal incidents and determination of events involving criminal conduct. Knowledge of the principles, application and implementation of a departmental philosophy of community-oriented policing. Knowledge of police interviewing and interrogation methods and procedures. Ability to physically and mentally react in a variety of emergency situations. Good social and general intelligence with the ability to communicate effectively both orally and in writing. Ability to understand and carry out oral and written instructions. Ability to drive an automobile safely and to use good judgment in its operations and use. Considerable skill in the use of Department approved firearms and other law enforcement equipment including, but not limited to, communications and alcohol/drug testing equipment. Physical ability to react to a variety of emergency law enforcement situations including juvenile crime, automobile accidents, and domestic disturbances and to use the best effort but least force needed to restore peace and order. Ability to observe situations, to report and record them clearly and accurately. Skills Considered Essential for Success -Knowledge of the organization and administration of higher education. -Knowledge of state, federal, and university rules, regulations and procedures. -Ability to work in a diverse and dynamic environment. -Ability to work collaboratively and independently with common goals as a part of a team. -Ability to make sound judgments. -Effective interpersonal communication, writing, and organizational abilities. -Civility in all interactions. -Demonstrate effective follow-up. Required Education and/or Experience The formal education of a high school diploma is required. A minimum of three years of experience as a police officer with a minimum of one as a police officer at UCA . Must be in good standing with the department and not currently on disciplinary probation. Preferred Education and/or Experience Equivalent to graduation two years of college in a criminal justice discipline or related field is preferred. University, Campus, and Community Description Thank you for your interest in employment at the University of Central Arkansas. Founded in 1907, UCA has an enrollment of more than 10,000 students and employs over 1,300 full-time employees. The university offers more than 85 undergraduate and 59 graduate certificate and degree programs, including doctoral programs in five disciplines. UCA is proud of its tradition of teaching excellence and its contributions in research, scholarship, and creative activities. Desired start date 01/01/2027 Position Summary and Qualifications License(s)/Certifications Physical Demands Checklist Sitting, Kneeling, Standing, Balancing, Lifting/Carrying, Walking, Climbing, Crawling Physical Exertion Primarily outdoor work Posting Detail Information Background Check Statement This position may be subject to a pre-employment criminal, sex-offender registry, financial history background check and drug screening. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication and/or adverse financial history alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background checks and drug screening information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. UCA EEO Statement The University of Central Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. In keeping with its nondiscrimination policy in employment, admissions and other functions and programs, the university considers employees and students on the basis of individual merit and will not discriminate against a person on the basis of gender, race or color, ethnicity, religion, spiritual beliefs, national origin, age, familial status, socioeconomic background, sexual orientation, disability, political beliefs, intellectual perspective, genetic information, military status, or other factors irrelevant to participation in its programs. Open Date 11/23/2026 Close Date 12/08/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicants Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you a current UCA employee? Yes No * Are you currently a certified police officer? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Optional Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Additional Documents University of Central Arkansas Human Resources 201 Donaghey Ave. Wingo Hall Conway, AR 72035 1.501.450.3181 1.501.450.5088 (fax) humanresources@uca.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.uca.edu/postings/20015": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Management (Organizational Behavior), tenure track",
      "description": "University of Central Arkansas Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor of Management (Organizational Behavior), tenure track Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs NEW Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor of Management (Organizational Behavior), tenure track Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. All applicants should include the following documents: (1) a detailed curriculum vitae, (2) a letter of application, (3) a statement of teaching philosophy, and (4) a research philosophy. Additionally, please provide contact information for three letters of reference. Position Details Position Information Posting Number F0652026 Working Title Assistant Professor of Management (Organizational Behavior), tenure track Department Org 314000 - Marketing and Management Why should you work at UCA? We are excited that you are considering joining the UCA Family—the faculty, staff, students and alumni—who make the University of Central Arkansas a great place to work! At the University of Central Arkansas, we value our employees striving to maintain a work-life balance. We realize that this affects the overall well-being of employees while learning to prioritize challenging work and the ability to make a difference. Participation in university-wide flexible work schedules may be available for certain positions. Training is provided on systems or job-specific software, and tools are available supporting professional and personal development. The benefits package allows you to customize your benefits to meet your current needs, with the ability to adjust them as your priorities change. This includes university contributions to health, dental, life and disability insurance, 80% tuition waivers for employees and their families, 12 official holidays, immediate leave accrual, and a retirement program with university contributions of 10% of employee salary. Also available are health and wellness incentives, as well as access to sports and cultural activities on campus for the family, free gym membership and equipment rentals. Position Summary The University of Central Arkansas Assistant Professor is responsible for teaching, research and service. This position is governed by state and federal laws and agency/institution policy. The position is tenure track and a nine-month appointment. Job Duties and Responsibilities Candidates should be able to teach courses in organizational behavior and conduct relevant research. A research emphasis related to AI or technology is desirable. Strong candidates will be willing and able to teach a suitable range of classes, maintain a program of research appropriate for college AACSB standards, and provide appropriate service. Responsibilities include: Provide quality teaching in the area of organizational behavior and related areas of expertise in face-to-face and online formats Teaching load is twelve hours per term with three hours of research release time (3/3 teaching load for the tenure-track role) for both fall and spring semesters The option to teach in the summer is often available, pursuant to student demand Produce quality research (as applicable) leading to published scholarly contributions in accordance with departmental and college expectations related to AACSB accreditation Provide service to the department, college, and academic community that is commensurate with rank and experience and aligned with expertise Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Applicants should possess a command of the discipline, the ability to deliver effective instruction and assess student performance in both face-to-face and online formats, and the capability of engaging in successful individual and collaborative research and scholarship relevant to their discipline that fits with AACSB standards. Skills Considered Essential for Success -Knowledge of the organization and administration of higher education. -Knowledge of state, federal, and university rules, regulations and procedures. -Ability to work in a diverse and dynamic environment. -Ability to work collaboratively and independently with common goals as a part of a team. -Ability to make sound judgments. -Effective interpersonal communication, writing, and organizational abilities. -Civility in all interactions. -Demonstrate effective follow-up. Required Education and/or Experience Candidates should hold a DBA or Ph.D. in Management or Business Administration ( ABD considered) or a closely related field. Preferred Education and/or Experience Professional experience is desirable, as well as teaching experience in either traditional or non-traditional settings. License(s)/Certifications (Required or Preferred) University, Campus, and Community Description Thank you for your interest in employment at the University of Central Arkansas. Founded in 1907, UCA has an enrollment of more than 10,000 students and employs over 1,300 full-time employees. The university offers more than 85 undergraduate and 59 graduate certificate and degree programs, including doctoral programs in five disciplines. UCA is proud of its tradition of teaching excellence and its contributions in research, scholarship, and creative activities. Desired start date 08/16/2027 Will this position be Onsite, Remote, or Hybrid? Onsite Posting Detail Information Background Check Statement This position may be subject to a pre-employment criminal, sex-offender registry, financial history background check and drug screening. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication and/or adverse financial history alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background checks and drug screening information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. UCA EEO Statement The University of Central Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. In keeping with its nondiscrimination policy in employment, admissions and other functions and programs, the university considers employees and students on the basis of individual merit and will not discriminate against a person on the basis of gender, race or color, ethnicity, religion, spiritual beliefs, national origin, age, familial status, socioeconomic background, sexual orientation, disability, political beliefs, intellectual perspective, genetic information, military status, or other factors irrelevant to participation in its programs. Open Date 11/25/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions to Applicants All applicants should include the following documents: (1) a detailed curriculum vitae, (2) a letter of application, (3) a statement of teaching philosophy, and (4) a research philosophy. Additionally, please provide contact information for three letters of reference. Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Application Teaching Philosophy Research Statement Optional Documents University of Central Arkansas Human Resources 201 Donaghey Ave. Wingo Hall Conway, AR 72035 1.501.450.3181 1.501.450.5088 (fax) humanresources@uca.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
